# Scenario snapshot

Two presentation-ready tables (No conversion, With conversion) covering every
year of the plan, about six in view, styled after the planning deck's "Assets,
Income & Taxes" slide. They scroll sideways with a synced bar on top and
bottom (`DualScroll`); row labels are sticky. One "Jump to" picker scrolls
both to a year.

- `snapshotTable.ts` is pure: it only reads `ProjectionRow` fields, never
  recomputes money. Keep it that way.
- Sections: assets, income, cash flow. Assets come in three groups, each
  tinted (`ASSET_GROUP_ROW`) and closed by a darker total row:
  **retirement assets** (each tax-deferred account, in
  `accountsInDisplayOrder`), **regular investments** (each after-tax account,
  real-estate equity, and each business from `household.businesses`), and
  **Roth** (each Roth account, the engine's implicit Roth when a plan converts
  without one, the Roth total, then a yearly conversion row only when the
  scenario converts). The Roth group always shows; the other two only when
  they have rows. A final **Total assets** row (no group, `subtotal: true`,
  darkest navy tint) adds up all three groups. Lines carry `group` and
  `subtotal`; other sections leave them unset. Income (monthly, per
  source, RMD rows kept when they pay anywhere in the projection), cash flow
  (monthly; a deposits row shows when the plan has deposits, since surplus
  nets them).
- It sits above the year-by-year table: nothing may go below that table in
  the results scroller.

## Retirement analysis charts

`PresentationCharts.tsx` (data in `presentationCharts.ts`) tells the snapshot
as four report-style pages with a No conversion / With conversion toggle:
need (spending + income tax + deposits into accounts per year), income sources (not drawn from the
household's accounts), those sources applied to the need, and the result once
RMDs and withdrawals are added. Applied income fills the need in
`INCOME_CATEGORIES` order and never exceeds it, so a year's shortfall equals
the table's negative surplus (deposits are part of the need, as Surplus nets
them). "Capitalized" is present value
at the balance-weighted average account growth rate (5% if no balances).
